ModelEvidence — Is That Spectral Line Real?
==========================================

Open index.html directly, or serve it with TeachingTools alongside. The Canvas
lesson uses JavaScript only: no Shiny, WebR, runtime downloads or data upload.
app.R and model.R are unchanged reference implementations.

Model: exact, normalised evidence
--------------------------------
There are 70 independent Gaussian flux measurements from 500 to 510 nm:
  flux_i ~ Normal(1 + A*g_i, sigma^2)
  g_i = exp[-0.5*((wavelength_i-505)/0.35)^2].
Continuum, centre, width and noise SD are known. H0 fixes A=0. H1 has the
PROPER Gaussian amplitude prior A ~ Normal(0,w^2), allowing both signs.
The finite positive SD is explicit (0.1–10); the Gaussian's SUPPORT is
unbounded. This is not a finite-support uniform prior, an unnormalised
Gaussian, or an improper flat-prior limit.

Ahat = sum[g_i*(flux_i-1)]/sum(g_i^2), s = sigma/sqrt(sum(g_i^2)).
Under H0, Ahat ~ Normal(0,s^2); under H1, Ahat ~ Normal(0,s^2+w^2).
Their predictive-density ratio at Ahat is BF10 exactly. Both predictive
densities have unit total area; they are not rescaled to their peaks/window.

The port also reports full-spectrum log evidence densities. If RSS is the
residual sum of squares around the best-fit amplitude, the shared factor is
  log C = -n/2 log(2*pi*sigma^2) - RSS/(2*sigma^2)
          + 0.5 log(2*pi*s^2).
log Z0 = log C + log Normal(Ahat|0,s^2);
log Z1 = log C + log Normal(Ahat|0,s^2+w^2).
The shared residual factor cancels in the ratio. The maximum likelihood ratio
instead has log value 0.5*(Ahat/s)^2 and is NOT used as model evidence.

Model probability assumes equal prior model odds. The page explains proper
normalisation, the Occam trade-off, possible absorption, probability rounding,
and why results do not account for searching over unknown line centres.
Original controls, presets and guides remain. No animation is introduced:
this lesson compares two static predictive models for one fixed spectrum.

Intentional differences
-----------------------
Seeded browser draws use Astro.createRng rather than R's RNG and therefore
are not bitwise-identical to R. Full-spectrum evidences, predictive log
densities and the conditional H1 amplitude posterior are added transparently.
The predictive grid resolves the narrow null even for a broad alternative.
The alternative prior is exactly the retained R model, not an approximation
or a substituted bounded prior. Changing it never changes data or the fit.

Numerical API / checks
---------------------
CommonJS require("./model.js") / browser ModelEvidenceModel exports defaults,
lineEvidence(estimate,error,priorSd), and compute(parameters). The latter
returns the spectrum, template, amplitude estimate/SE, evidence summaries and
fully normalised logEvidenceNull/logEvidenceLine. lineEvidence also gives
log predictive densities, log Bayes factor, equal-odds probability,
log maximum likelihood ratio and the H1 amplitude posterior mean/SD.
Input validation uses Astro.ValidationError in both browser and Node.

Run from the repository root with Node 22+:
  node --test ModelEvidence/test_model.js
  TMPDIR="$PWD/ModelEvidence" node ModelEvidence/test_browser.js

Checks include independent R fixtures, the analytic zero-estimate Occam
factor, sign symmetry, the narrow-prior null limit, numerical quadrature of
likelihood times prior, full-spectrum multivariate-normal normalisation,
data/fit invariance under prior changes, seeded stability and boundaries.
Browser tests check both Canvas panels, unchanged spectra, finite boundary
results and matching full/predictive evidence ratios. Shared checks cover
presets, reset, invalid seed, tabs, responsive layout and file:// loading.

Runtime assets (no others)
-------------------------
index.html, model.js, app.js, ../TeachingTools/statistics.js,
../TeachingTools/browser.js, ../TeachingTools/browser.css,
